      <!-- FAQ SECTION -->
      <section class="section" id="faq" style="border-top: 2px solid var(--border); padding-top: 40px; margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;">
          
          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">1. What is the primary function of chelating builders in phosphate-free dishwasher detergents?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Phosphate-free dishwasher formulations rely on chelating agents like Sodium Citrate, GLDA (glutamic acid diacetic acid), and MGDA (methylglycinediacetic acid) to sequester calcium (Ca2+) and magnesium (Mg2+) ions in hard water. By binding these multivalent cations, builders prevent them from precipitating with anionic surfactants or forming insoluble mineral scales (filming/spotting) on glassware and stainless steel surfaces.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">2. Why must surfactants used in automatic dishwashing have a low cloud point?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Automatic dishwashers operate under high mechanical shear from rotating spray arms. Traditional high-foaming anionic surfactants would generate excessive foam, causing pump cavitation or foam lock, which halts water pressure. Low-foaming nonionic surfactants, such as EO/PO block copolymers, have a specific "cloud point" below the wash operating temperature (typically 40-60°C). Above the cloud point, these surfactants become insoluble, phase-separating to act as highly efficient defoamers, while remaining excellent surface wetting and grease-emulsifying agents.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">3. How do proteases and amylases cooperate in a dishwasher formulation?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Proteases and amylases target distinct biological soils. Bacterial proteases (e.g., subtilisins) hydrolyze baked-on protein deposits (egg, meat, milk) into soluble amino acids and peptides. Alpha-amylases target gelatinized starch residues (pasta, potatoes, rice), cleaving the alpha-1,4-glucosidic bonds to convert starch into soluble dextrins and sugars. Together, they break down complex organic matrices that normal surfactants cannot solubilize at standard wash times.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">4. What is the mechanism of the active oxygen bleaching system in dishwasher tablets?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              The oxygen bleach system consists of a solid peroxide carrier, primarily sodium percarbonate, paired with an activator like tetraacetylethylenediamine (TAED). When dissolved in water at temperatures above 40°C, the sodium percarbonate releases hydrogen peroxide. The hydrogen peroxide immediately reacts with TAED to generate peracetic acid. Peracetic acid is a highly powerful, low-temperature oxidizing agent that chemically breaks down chromophores in colored stains (such as tea, coffee, and fruit juices), making them colorless and soluble.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">5. How do polycarboxylates prevent filming and spotting on glassware?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Polycarboxylates are water-soluble acrylic acid polymers that act as threshold mineral inhibitors and crystal growth modifiers. They adsorb onto the faces of growing calcium carbonate crystals, distorting their crystalline structures and preventing them from aggregating or adhering to surfaces. This threshold inhibition keeps calcium carbonate suspended in the wash liquor as a colloidal dispersion, preventing it from forming a visible white film or spots on dishes.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="box" style="margin: 0; background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); padding: 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-md);">
            <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">6. Why does a separate rinse aid need to have an acidic pH?</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
              Rinse aids are injected during the final rinse cycle. They are formulated with organic acids (such as citric acid) to neutralize any alkaline residues carried over from the wash cycle (buffering the pH down to 3.0-5.0). An acidic pH also ensures that any residual hard-water minerals remain highly soluble and do not precipitate. This is combined with ultra-low surface tension nonionic surfactants to cause the water to drain as a continuous thin sheet (sheet-flow), enabling spotless rapid drying.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
